<WorkBlock id="responsibility">

## Responsibility

I was the sole designer. I owned the product experience, interface design, visual direction, and Cardinal design system. I spent my first month learning the traffic-planning domain, auditing the existing product, interviewing Quality Counts staff, and contacting cities that had already seen a DataPoint demonstration.

Quality Counts supplied domain and business context. I partnered with its internal team and the contracted development team as they implemented the product. Product scope was collaborative; I did not write the production application.

</WorkBlock>

<WorkBlock id="decision-01">

## Decision 01: Make the product native to the web

**The problem:** Traffic engineers often used desktop software built around older Windows interaction patterns, while residents expected something closer to the map products they already knew.

**What I chose:** Treat DataPoint as a web product instead of recreating a desktop application in the browser.

**Why:** It would work from any connected computer and could use familiar web patterns for navigation, search, filtering, and progressive disclosure.

**The tradeoff:** We could not copy expert workflows directly from legacy tools. We had to decide which controls belonged in the default interface and which could wait until a deeper analysis view.

**What informed it:** Competitive analysis, an audit of the original product, and conversations with Quality Counts and prospective city customers. “Modern” and browser-based access came up repeatedly.

</WorkBlock>

<WorkBlock id="decision-02">

## Decision 02: Separate overview from analysis

**The problem:** Some customers wanted a quick status check. Others needed count files, turning movements, peak periods, vehicle types, and capacity controls.

**What I chose:** Organize DataPoint 2.0 around a search and filter bar, the map, and a contextual inspector. Selecting a station opened deeper analysis in a modal, which could move to a separate browser tab for comparison.

**Why:** The map and inspector kept the most common information close at hand. The modal made room for detailed analysis without crowding the default view with every control.

**The tradeoff:** The modal only worked with one station at a time. People still needed separate tabs to compare several stations.

**What shipped:** People could browse visible stations, inspect one, and move into intersection or midblock analysis without losing the map as their starting point.

</WorkBlock>

