<WorkBlock id="validation">

## Validation

I studied the existing product and traffic-planning domain, interviewed Quality Counts staff, contacted cities that had seen a product demonstration, and compared the tools used by engineers with map products familiar to residents.

That work shaped the scope, the three-part layout, and the way we revealed advanced controls. We did not collect reliable metrics for task success, support volume, or implementation time, so I cannot make claims about them.

</WorkBlock>

<WorkBlock id="outcome">

## Outcome

The team shipped DataPoint 2.0, its capacity-planning workflow, and the Cardinal design system. When I last spoke with Quality Counts, about 30 customers were waiting to be onboarded. That showed demand, but it did not tell us anything about adoption, retention, or task efficiency.

Cardinal also gave the team a shared starting point for testing other product ideas. We did not measure how much time it saved or how consistently the team used it.

</WorkBlock>

<WorkBlock id="reflection">

## What I would do next

The decision that held up best was separating quick checks from detailed analysis. Even expert users benefit from a clear default view when advanced controls are easy to find.

I would now prioritize three follow-up areas:

1. Build internal tools to validate and clean historical data before import.
2. Test a unified DataPoint instance so cities can share traffic data without separate product URLs. I proposed this direction, but the team postponed it beyond the 2.0 release.
3. Test windowing or minimized stations for side-by-side comparison instead of relying on separate browser tabs.

I would measure onboarding completion, time to find a station, errors during data import, use of basic versus advanced capacity controls, and the number of stations compared during a planning task.

</WorkBlock>

